---
/**
 * /guides/meso-saving - economy fundamentals for new players. The
 * "why am I always broke" answer.
 *
 * SEO target: 'maplestory classic meso saving', 'how to make meso
 * early maplestory', 'maplestory meso guide beginner'. All fresh
 * MSCW-specific queries that will spike hard around launch.
 */

import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Meso Saving Guide - Liven's Classic Maple Guide"
	description="Where your first million mesos go, and where they shouldn't. Potion overspending, gear rebuying, scroll gambling - the three sinks that keep new MapleStory Classic players broke."
	currentPath="/guides"
	theme="kerning"
	decorations={false}
>
	<section class="section-heading">
		<div>
			<span class="eyebrow eyebrow--guide">Economy</span>
			<h1>Meso Saving Guide</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Where your first million mesos go, and where they shouldn't.
				Three sinks kill new-player wallets: potion overspending, gear
				rebuying, and scroll gambling. This guide covers how to avoid
				each and where to actually spend meso for maximum return.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<article class="prose">
		<h2 id="why-meso-matters">Why meso matters early</h2>
		<p>
			MapleStory Classic's economy is deliberately tight from Lv 1 to
			30. Mob drop rates on meso are low, quest rewards are modest, and
			the two biggest early-game costs (potions and gear rebuys) compound
			fast. A new player who spends recklessly through Lv 20 will hit a
			wall at 2nd job advancement (Lv 30) with no meso for the
			job-change weapon and no path forward except grinding for it.
		</p>
		<p>
			A player who saves through the same range hits Lv 30 with 200-500k
			banked, buys the class weapon, tips a partymate for KPQ carries, and
			starts scrolling their first real gear piece. Same time
			investment - dramatically different position.
		</p>

		<h2 id="the-three-sinks">The three sinks that kill wallets</h2>

		<h3>Sink 1: Potion overspending</h3>
		<p>
			The single biggest early-game meso leak. New players buy the
			shiniest potion available and spam it constantly. Reality: below
			Lv 30, you don't need premium potions.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Lv 1-15:</strong> Red Potions (300 HP) at 30 meso each
				from any grocer. Buy them 100 at a time. Never touch anything
				higher-tier.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Lv 15-25:</strong> Orange Potions (500 HP) at 100 meso.
				Only start using Orange when Red Potion single-use no longer
				restores you above 50% HP.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Lv 25-35:</strong> White Potions (1000 HP) at 240 meso.
				This is where most players stay through 2nd-job training.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Lv 35-50:</strong> Mana Elixirs / Elixirs from Herb
				shops. Priced 400-600 meso each; heal 2000+ HP.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p class="callout callout--red">
			<strong>The overspending trap:</strong> a player who Orange-Potions
			the whole Lv 1-20 range burns roughly <strong>4x more meso on
			potions</strong> than a player who sticks to Red Potions. That's
			the gap between "broke at Lv 30" and "job-change weapon
			purchased".
		</p>

		<h3>Sink 2: Gear rebuying</h3>
		<p>
			Buying "upgrade" gear every 3-5 levels sounds smart. It is not.
			Every mid-tier NPC gear piece you buy is meso that could've saved
			for one <em>real</em> upgrade at Lv 30 or 40.
		</p>
		<p>The rule: <strong>buy gear at level bands, not every level.</strong></p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Lv 1-10:</strong> Wear whatever Maple Island quests
				give you. Don't buy NPC gear on the island.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Lv 10-20:</strong> One armor set from your class town's
				NPC (Perion Warriors, Ellinia Mages, etc.) + a Maple weapon or
				quest weapon. Total spend: ~5-15k meso.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Lv 20-30:</strong> Wait for KPQ drops (Rock/Paper/Scissors
				gloves + shoes). Don't NPC-buy a replacement while you're PQing.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Lv 30:</strong> First real spend - buy your class's
				job-change weapon from the appropriate town NPC. This is the
				weapon you'll use for 10-20 levels.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Lv 35-50:</strong> Player-market (FM) gear starts
				outperforming NPC gear. Check <a href="/items">items DB</a>
				and FM stalls before buying NPC.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>Sink 3: Scroll gambling</h3>
		<p>
			Scrolls are consumable items that upgrade equipment. 30% scrolls
			give big stats but fail 70% of the time (item survives, slot
			consumed). 60% scrolls are middle-ground. 100% scrolls always
			succeed but give small gains.
		</p>
		<p>
			New players buy expensive 30% scrolls, fail 6 in a row on a
			borrowed weapon, and burn a week of meso in one FM session.
			<strong>Do not scroll gear you plan to replace inside 10
			levels.</strong> Do not scroll clean weapons you don't own outright.
			Do not scroll with 30% variants until you have backup meso
			specifically labeled "scroll budget I can afford to lose".
		</p>
		<p class="callout callout--yellow">
			<strong>New-player scroll rule:</strong> only use <strong>100%
			scrolls</strong> until Lv 50+. They're cheap, they always work,
			and the smaller stat gain compounds fine at low levels. Save
			30%/60% for gear you plan to keep long-term.
		</p>

		<h2 id="priority-spending">Priority spending order</h2>
		<p>
			If you have 100k meso in the bank at Lv 25, spend it in this order:
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<strong>Full potion stack for your training range</strong> -
				100 potions at the right tier. Never grind down to your last
				potion; teleport-scroll home if needed.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Repair / replace shoes and gloves</strong> if lost from
				death. Cheapest slots to rebuy.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Return scrolls</strong> from any grocer - 1000 meso
				each, save 5-10% EXP on death by teleporting to town before it
				triggers. Cheap insurance.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Bank the rest for Lv 30 weapon purchase.</strong> Class
				weapons cost 15-80k depending on class. Have the meso ready.
			</li>
		</ol>

		<h2 id="how-to-earn">How to actually earn meso</h2>
		<p>Three reliable paths in Classic:</p>

		<h3>1. Quest rewards (Lv 1-30)</h3>
		<p>
			Every town has ~20-40 quests, most reward mesos + items. The Maple
			Island chain alone gives 5-8k. Victoria Island's town quests hand
			out 20-50k across the region if you clear the list. Do quests
			WHILE training in an area - the reward makes the grind roughly 30%
			more profitable.
		</p>

		<h3>2. ETC item selling (all levels)</h3>
		<p>
			Every mob drops "Etc" items (screws, oars, feathers, etc.). Most
			are worth 1-30 meso individually - not much. But at high mob
			density in a good training map, ETC drops can equal 40-60% of
			your total meso income. Never delete ETC drops until you've
			checked their NPC sell price. Some ETC (e.g. Wolf Skulls) are
			quest items - check the <a href="/items">items DB</a>.
		</p>

		<h3>3. Party quest rewards (Lv 21-50)</h3>
		<p>
			KPQ gives 30-50k meso per successful run from bonus stage + boss
			drops. LPQ at Lv 35 gives 100k+ per run once you factor in Broken
			Glasses (sells for 150k+ each in FM). PQing is not just faster
			EXP - it's better meso per hour than solo grinding for these
			levels.
		</p>

		<h2 id="merch-basics">Merching basics (Lv 30+)</h2>
		<p>
			Free Market merchanting - buying player items low and reselling
			high - is the wealth-building path in Classic once you have a
			meso baseline. It's not required, but understanding it early
			pays off.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Get a Store Permit at Lv 30+.</strong> Costs 5m meso
				from the FM entrance NPC. Lets you set up a stall for AFK
				selling.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Watch FM channel 1 chat for "WTS" and "WTB" spam.</strong>
				Note prices for items you recognize. Patterns emerge - certain
				scrolls consistently underprice on channels 5-10 vs channel 1.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Focus on high-turnover items:</strong> 60% Overall for
				DEX scrolls, 60% Cape for WATK, quest-rewards NPCs pay well for
				(Zombie's Lost Gold Tooth, Firebrand from Skeleton mobs).
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Never merch what you don't recognize.</strong> Bidding
				on a Godly Meba Cape you can't price-check is how new merchers
				lose 10m in one afternoon.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="milestones">Meso milestones to aim for</h2>
		<div class="milestones">
			<div class="milestone">
				<strong>50k</strong>
				<span>by Lv 20 - covers potions to Lv 30</span>
			</div>
			<div class="milestone">
				<strong>200k</strong>
				<span>by Lv 30 - job-change weapon + potion stock</span>
			</div>
			<div class="milestone">
				<strong>1m</strong>
				<span>by Lv 40 - first real armor/cape scroll investment</span>
			</div>
			<div class="milestone">
				<strong>5m</strong>
				<span>by Lv 50 - Store Permit + first FM merch inventory</span>
			</div>
			<div class="milestone">
				<strong>50m+</strong>
				<span>by Lv 70 - endgame gear investment path opens</span>
			</div>
		</div>

		<h2 id="what-not-to-do">What NOT to do</h2>
		<ul class="dont-list">
			<li>
				<strong>Don't buy the Cash Shop pet purely to save meso.</strong>
				A pet costs 15k NX (real money) and its auto-loot feature only
				saves you the meso you'd lose from missed drops. Do the math
				before you spend real money.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Don't trade unknown players your good scrolls.</strong>
				Chaos Scrolls and 60% weapon scrolls have well-known FM prices.
				If someone offers a "deal" that beats FM channel 1 by 50%,
				it's usually a scam via a mislabeled ETC item in trade.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Don't chase the perfect item early.</strong> A
				"godly" Zakum Helmet costs 500m+. Getting one at Lv 30 is
				pointless - you can't equip Zakum Helmet until Lv 50 anyway.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Don't AP-reset until you understand the math.</strong>
				AP resets cost NX (real money). HP washing is a Lv 100+
				endgame concern; you do not need it in your first 50 levels.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="whats-next">Where to go next</h2>
		<ul class="next-links">
			<li><a href="/guides/glossary">Glossary</a> - decode "60% CS" and "godly WATK scroll"</li>
			<li><a href="/guides/getting-started">Getting Started</a> - the full new-player walkthrough</li>
			<li><a href="/items">Items DB</a> - price-check and quest-check any drop</li>
			<li><a href="/party-quests/kerning-pq">Kerning PQ</a> - the reliable Lv 21-30 meso path</li>
		</ul>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.section-heading {
		max-width: 68ch;
		margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		color: var(--text-body);
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
	}

	.prose {
		max-width: 72ch;
		color: var(--text-body);
		line-height: 1.6;
	}
	.prose h2 {
		color: var(--text-heading);
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		margin: 2rem 0 0.6rem;
		padding-bottom: 0.3rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		scroll-margin-top: 5rem;
	}
	.prose h3 {
		color: var(--text-heading);
		font-size: 1.15rem;
		margin: 1.4rem 0 0.4rem;
	}
	.prose p {
		margin: 0.7rem 0;
	}
	.prose ul, .prose ol {
		margin: 0.7rem 0;
		padding-left: 1.4rem;
	}
	.prose li {
		margin: 0.35rem 0;
	}
	.prose a {
		color: var(--accent-primary);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.callout {
		margin: 1.2rem 0 !important;
		padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
		background: var(--bg-cloud);
		border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-primary);
		border-radius: 0.35rem;
	}
	.callout--red { border-left-color: #c94a4a; }
	.callout--yellow { border-left-color: #b8891a; }

	.milestones {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr));
		gap: 0.6rem;
		margin: 1rem 0;
	}
	.milestone {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.2rem;
		padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem;
		background: var(--surface-card);
		border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		border-left: 4px solid var(--accent-primary);
		border-radius: 0.4rem;
	}
	.milestone strong {
		font-size: 1.3rem;
		color: var(--accent-primary);
	}
	.milestone span {
		font-size: 0.82rem;
		color: var(--text-body);
	}

	.dont-list li strong {
		color: var(--text-heading);
	}

	.next-links {
		list-style: none;
		padding: 0 !important;
	}
	.next-links li {
		padding: 0.5rem 0.8rem;
		background: var(--surface-card);
		border: 1px solid var(--border-card);
		border-radius: 0.35rem;
		margin: 0.35rem 0 !important;
	}
</style>
